  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-122473",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨12{t}^{2},-{e}^{-t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 12{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (-{e}^{-t})\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3}+{C}_{1},{e}^{-t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨0,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨4({0}^{3})+{C}_{1},{e}^{-0}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,1⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨4{t}^{3},{e}^{-t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨4{t}^{3},{e}^{-t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{4}+{C}_{3},-{e}^{-t}+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨-1,-4⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{0}^{4}+{C}_{3},-{e}^{-0}+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,-4⟩ & =⟨{C}_{3},-1+{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}=-1$ and ${C}_{4}=-3.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{t}^{4}-1,-{e}^{-t}-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
